Apple has recently made some huge  changes to the MacBook lineup and a lot  of you may be wondering what exactly is  different between each MacBook and which  one is the right fit for you so today  we've got apples complete lineup of 2019  mac books right here in the office which  should mostly say the same into 2020  we've got the MacBook Air for $1100  we've got the base 13-inch MacBook Pro  for $1300 the higher-end 13-inch Pro for  $1,800 and the 15-inch MacBook Pro for  $2,400 and if you're watching this video  a couple of months after I made it  chances are there also may be a 16 inch  macbook pro available but as of today  July 27 2009 teen it's still only a  rumor and it's gonna be a very high-end  laptop like over $3,000 at the base  price so it'll be in a league of its own  but if you're curious about that model  check out our channel for videos on that  now let's get started with comparing  literally everything we can between all  these Mac books starting with  portability the air is the lightest at  just 2.75 pounds but it's hardly  noticeable compared to the three-pound  13-inch pro from above the air is  basically just as large as a 13-inch  MacBook Pro so portability should be  pretty similar and much more portable  than the 15-inch the MacBook Pros also  have more of a flat slate shape compared  to the MacBook Air which has a wedge  design that gets thinner near the front  end each of these Mac books has a high  quality retina resolution display but  the AERS display isn't as nice it's  quite a bit dimmer than the pros that  each have an incredible 500 nits of  brightness and it's definitely not as  color accurate since every MacBook Pro  has P 3 wide color gamut which has about  25% more colors than 100% srgb which is  a must-have for photo editors as far as  display sharpness they all have the same  retina quality 227 pixels per inch and  this year every MacBook and the lineup  finally comes with true tone technology  which adjusts the white balance of the  display to match the environment around  you speaker quality and volume is a huge  deal for some people especially to me so  here's the sound comparisons you can  hear the differences for yourself  [Music]  [Music]  we noticed that the air has slightly  worse speakers than the base search and  inch pro but volumes sound quality and  bass go way up with the $1,800 13-inch  Pro as well as the 15-inch model so if  speaker quality matters to you you  should consider the higher-end models  however the air sounds better and louder  than almost every Windows laptop we've  tested now on to the keyboards they're  all packing Apple's latest  fourth-generation butterfly keys that  have new key switch materials which  should be more reliable than before and  we've yet to hear of any issues with the  ports on keyboard but even if you do all  these Mac books come with a 4-year free  keyboard replacement warranty if you  have any issues with the keys apart from  that the MacBook Airs keyboard slopes  downward towards the front edge so it's  actually a bit more comfortable to type  on compared to the pros and this year  every single macbook in the line-up  comes with the touch ID sensor built  into the power button which makes it  really convenient to log in use Apple  pay online and install apps off the web  without having to enter your password  every macbook pro this year comes with  the touch bar an OLED display that  replaces the top row of keys and is  fully customizable however not many  people like it because it's more  difficult to do things like adjust  display brightness volume and use the  Escape key I personally don't really  like the touch bar but it's not that big  of a deal and this year every macbook  also comes with a t2 chip which helps  with security and provides Hardware  transcoding of heb see video footage and  now let's talk about the ports every  port on every map book this year  supports Thunderbolt 3 but both the  13-inch MacBook Air and the lower and  MacBook Pro come with only two of these  ports compared to four ports on the more  expensive pros if you're a basic user  that doesn't really connect to external  displays storage drives or external  devices very often you'll probably be  just fine with one of the two port  models and you also have the choice of  buying a Thunderbolt 3 hub just in case  you do need more ports but having 4  ports from the get-go is definitely more  convenient especially for those who use  those ports often now if you love large  trackpads the 15-inch pro has the  biggest one of them all followed by the  13-inch pro and then the MacBook Air  which has a slightly smaller trackpad  but is still larger than most Windows  laptops for battery life the new MacBook  Air is rated at 12 hours compared to 10  hours on the MacBook  so if battery life is what you're after  the air is the best option and if you  love maxing out the brightness on your  display like I do you could see an even  bigger difference since the air has a  dimmer display and finally let's talk  about performance starting with the  processors this year at the base 13 inch  MacBook Pro finally gets a quad-core  processor so the MacBook Air is now the  only one in the lineup with a dual-core  CPU the $1,800 MacBook Pro has a quad  core and the 15-inch pro has a 6 core  that can actually be upgraded to an 8  core if you're willing to spend $2,800  looking at RAW processing performance  using Geekbench force multi-core test we  can see that the MacBook Air with its  dual-core CPU is way behind the MacBook  Pros and surprisingly the $1300 pro  performs almost the same as the $1,800  model and the 15-inch gives you a pretty  good jump in performance as well so we  decided to look at performance per  dollar using the full retail price of  each macbook and the new $1300 based  macbook pro comes out way ahead with  incredible amounts of raw power for the  cache with the air being a very bad  value performance wise one thing that  you won't find on the spec sheet is that  the $1300 based MacBook Pro has only one  fan just like on the MacBook Air while  the more expensive $1,800 and $2,400  pros have duel fans something like this  could definitely impact performance for  longer workloads so we also ran  Cinebench R 20 a more realistic  benchmark which is long enough to heat  up the systems and see how well the  cooling system works and surprisingly  the single fan on the $1300 pro didn't  really limit performance like we thought  it would so we're really impressed with  it however we noticed that while the  dual fans on the higher-end models were  louder at first they eventually slowed  down a little bit as the system  stabilized but the single fan models  kept running at full blast until the end  of the test so the dual fan models will  probably be quieter overall we also  tested SSD speeds and here we see a  pretty big difference when going to the  more expensive MacBook Pros but even the  less expensive models are still fast  enough for most people especially basic  users so there you have it there are all  the 2018 MacBooks compared and if you're  still confused on which macbook you  should buy let me break it down for you  the $1300 based 13-inch MacBook Pro is  by far  the best value you can get in terms of  performance and overall MacBook  experience but if you don't really need  a great display or high performance and  you'd rather save a couple hundred bucks  you can definitely go for the MacBook  Air instead the air is great if you're a  writer a student or someone who just  wants a premium reliable laptop for  doing basic computer tasks like web  browsing working with documents and  sometimes watching some Netflix as well  as getting some extra battery life out  of it  so for basic use the MacBook Air will be  the best option for you now if you're  doing any kind of professional tasks  like photo editing video editing 3d  animation or anything like that and you  love having the best display you can get  with better speakers in the air then I  would highly recommend going for the  $1,300 MacBook Pro instead it's the best  value macbook we've ever seen now the  $1,800 13-inch MacBook Pro is for those  people who have a bit more cash left in  their budget and are willing to spend it  to get a few more luxury improvements  like an extra two Thunderbolt three  ports better speakers faster storage  speeds slightly faster performance and  two cooling fans which should lead to an  overall quieter laptop during heavy  workloads now all of those things aren't  really deal-breakers but if they matter  to you you should consider that model it  already comes with 256 gigs of storage  so in reality it's $300 more than the  base model with a matching SSD and  finally the 15-inch MacBook Pro is for  those who know that they need the extra  performance from the six core CPU are  even upgrading to the eight core and if  you're a serious 4k or raw Video Editor  this is the one again since it has  dedicated graphics and it's also the  only model that can come with 32 gigs of  ram so it's great for high-end photo  editors or programmers it's for the  people who want the absolute best with  the biggest trackpad the best speakers  the biggest display and the highest  performance you can get and to finish  this video off here are some general  purchasing recommendations if you're  anything more than a basic user we'd  recommend getting at least 256 gigabytes  of storage and if you're working with  professional tasks almost every day we'd  also recommend getting 16 gigabytes of  RAM especially if you're a photo editor  we also recommend that you never upgrade  the processor unless you're getting  extra cores out of the deal which is  only available on the 15 inch going from  6 cores  eight-course upgrading from an i5 to an  i7 doesn't change anything  other than a slight performance boost  which almost always isn't worth the  price and if you're a very serious Video  Editor get the best graphics you can  afford especially the Vega 20 graphics  and for photo editors graphics will do  basically nothing for you so you should  focus on RAM first and then the  processor and if the 16 inch MacBook Pro  is out already check out one of those  two videos right there to see is  comparing it to the 15-inch model if  this video helps you out  tap the like button and click the circle  above to subscribe thanks for watching  and we'll see you in the next one  [Music]

